Ladybird aligns column flex items using baselines measured along the
horizontal cross axis. Horizontal text synthesizes a border-edge
baseline; orthogonal text supplies a content baseline. Compatible
first- and last-baseline groups align from their respective physical
edges, and their combined extents contribute to a wrapped line’s size.
Scroll-container baselines are clamped to the border edges. Baselines
exported to the parent remain independent of cross-axis alignment.

Blink selects the baseline set in FlexLayoutAlgorithm::BaselineAscent
and SynthesizedBaselineAscent. These functions transform its distance
into the flex axis, and include the corresponding margin. The helpers
DetermineBaselineWritingMode and DetermineBaselineGroup select the
axis and sharing group. LogicalBoxFragment supplies natural or
synthesized baselines, and clamps scroll-container baselines.

Gecko’s FlexItem::BaselineOffsetFromOuterCrossEdge synthesizes a
baseline when the item’s block axis is the main axis. Otherwise, it
uses FlexItem::ResolvedAscent and converts the distance from the item’s
block-start edge to the requested cross edge. These decisions keep a
column item’s vertical baseline out of its horizontal placement.

WebKit selects baselines in FlexFormattingUtils, whose function
marginBoxAscentForFlexItem chooses natural or synthesized baselines
from the item and container axes, and clamps scrolling items. In
FlexFormattingContext, performBaselineAlignment places compatible
groups from the edge selected by the item’s block flow and first/last
baseline preference, after calculating their shared baseline.

The probes ran in Chrome 157.0.8103.78, Firefox 151.0, and WebKit 26.5.
All three place both reduced-case items at x=0, and pass all five
subtests of align-items-baseline-column-horz.html with x offsets 144,
159, 37, 22, and 27. Ladybird matches those results and the 119 retained
numeric regression cases’ Chrome results. Checks include reversed and
wrapped columns, RTL, first/last baselines, margins, borders, scrolling,
mixed writing modes, nested formatting contexts, and shifted inlines.

The engines disagree on negative margins, empty vertical items, some
middle-aligned atomic boxes, and mixed text orientations. Ladybird
matches Chrome in those retained cases. Separate sub/super probes
still expose Ladybird’s approximate font-based shifts. SerenitySans
middle alignment also retains a font-metric difference. These separate
metric rules aren’t changed. The sources were inspected for behavior;
their implementation structure and text weren’t copied.
